### 2026-08-25 — `create_figures.ipynb`: new Fig 5 (weighting's effect on generalization)
- **Trigger:** coworker feedback on the sent figures — they wanted "the first
  graph applied to the third graph": Fig 1's *difference* presentation
  (survey_weighted − standard) applied to Fig 4's *quantity* (test − population
  gap). i.e. does fitting with survey weights change how well the model
  generalizes to the population? The raw comparison already existed as Fig 4's
  grouped grey/accent bars; this makes the weighting *effect* explicit as one
  bar per metric.
- **New Fig 5 inserted right under Fig 4** (the generalization-gap cell), so the
  old proba-distributions figure renumbers `## Figure 5` → `## Figure 6`.
  **Notebook is now 6 figures:** 1 test-split deltas, 2 predicted vs actual
  rate, 3 confusion, 4 test→population gap, **5 Δ-generalization-gap**, 6 proba
  distributions.
- **What it shows.** Reuses `gen_df` built in the Fig 4 cell (so it must stay
  ordered after it): `gap_wide = gen_df.pivot(... columns=variant, values=gap)`,
  `delta = survey_weighted_gap − standard_gap`. 1×3 panels (per model), one
  horizontal ACCENT bar per metric, symmetric zoomed x-axis (`±|delta|.max()×1.45`
  — deltas are ~±0.005), dotted zero line, per-model titles, top-left suptitle,
  value labels. X-label spells the sign: "Survey-weighted − standard
  generalization gap (< 0 = weighting generalized better)". No color legend —
  single series, x-label carries it.
- **Finding (reinforces the 2026-08-11 Fig 4 note):** weighting barely moves
  generalization. Deltas are ~±0.005 across all three models; the only
  consistent pattern is survey-weighted trades a hair of accuracy/precision for
  a hair of recall (e.g. XGB accuracy −0.004, recall +0.005). ROC-AUC delta
  ≈ 0 everywhere. So the honest read for the coworker is "no material
  generalization benefit at this feature budget."
- **Same-axis caveat to flag if asked:** the gap uses `population_unweighted_*`
  only (weighted population metrics aren't persisted per-row), so this compares
  *fitting* regimes, not weighted-vs-unweighted *evaluation*.
- **Durability:** single atomic `json.dump` write (not sequential
  `NotebookEdit`, per the ExFAT-clobber lesson), `._*` sidecar swept first,
  then `sync` + re-read confirmed the 15-cell state stuck. **Verified headless**
  (Agg): all 8 code cells execute against the real APFS store + 20-iteration
  labeled parquets; rendered Fig 5 to PNG and eyeballed (no clipping, house
  style intact). Not opened in Jupyter.

