  the PR curves. 1×3 panels (per model), `calibration_curve(..., n_bins=10,
  strategy="quantile")` per variant on the pooled test rows, standard GREY /
  survey-weighted ACCENT, light-grey (`#b0b0b0`) dashed y=x reference (distinct
  from standard's GREY), `set_box_aspect(1)` square panels, fixed 0–1 axes for
  cross-model comparability. A local `ece()` helper (10 uniform bins,
  count-weighted) direct-labels each variant's ECE in the panel legend.
- **Finding (worth keeping):** the balanced class weighting makes all models
  *over-forecast* disability (reliability curves below the diagonal). LR
  standard is badly miscalibrated (ECE 0.330, mean proba 0.45 vs 12% base) and
  survey weighting fixes it a lot (ECE 0.074); XGB is moderate both ways and
  slightly *worse* survey-weighted (0.156 → 0.189, non-monotonic curve); NN is
  near-perfectly calibrated either way (ECE ~0.003–0.004, mean proba ≈ base
  rate). So the survey-weighting calibration effect is model-dependent, not a
  uniform improvement.
