### 2026-08-04 — all three drivers: 200k/50k split + population eval (new eval architecture)
- **User directive (with a diagram):** stop training on the whole per-state
  sample and evaluating on the rest of the file. Instead, out of the ~250k
  5000-per-state sample, split **200k train / 50k test**; build model M on the
  200k; record **weighted + unweighted** metrics on the 50k test split; then
  record **unweighted** metrics on the **rest of the population** (every row not
  in the sample). Goal is the bias contrast: weighted/unweighted test precision
  vs. population precision.
- **Sampling change.** The 5000-per-state draw is unchanged (~250k, one scan),
  but the sample is now `train_test_split(test_size=TEST_SIZE=50_000,
  stratify=y_all, random_state=SEED+it)` into `X`/`X_test` (200k/50k). The
  training block (random search, refit, `train_*` weighted+unweighted metrics)
  is otherwise untouched and now operates on the 200k. Renamed `train_idx`/
  `train_mask`/`train_df` → `sample_idx`/`sample_mask`/`sample_df`; the mask now
  covers the whole 250k sample so the population pass excludes both train and
  test (no overlap with the 50k test metrics). `balanced_spw` (XGB) and `cw`
  (NN) moved to after the split so they're computed on the 200k train `y`.
- **Three eval surfaces per iteration** (was two):
  - `{variant}_iter{N}_train` — unchanged (weighted + unweighted train metrics,
    nested search trials).
  - `{variant}_iter{N}_test` — the 50k split, now logs **both**
    `test_unweighted_*` and `test_weighted_*` (was unweighted-only). In-memory,
    no streaming.
  - `{variant}_iter{N}_population` — NEW. Streams the file (50k `BATCH`), skips
    `sample_mask` rows, logs `population_unweighted_*` only. This is the old
    streaming loop repointed from `train_mask` to `sample_mask`.
- **Labeled parquet is now the 50k test split** (was the full held-out set):
  `data/<stem>_labeled_<model>_iter{N}.parquet`, same 5-col proba+binary schema,
  50k rows. Population predictions are **not** persisted (only its metrics are
  logged) — the 33M-row proba dump per iteration wasn't worth the disk. Ask if
  a population-level calibration/confusion figure later needs those columns.
- **Bias itself is not computed in the drivers** — it's a subtraction of logged
  metrics (test weighted/unweighted precision − population precision), left for
  `create_figures.ipynb`. That notebook is already stale (per earlier entries)
  and now also needs the new run names + `population_*` / `test_weighted_*` keys.
- **Cost:** still 2 full passes over the file per iteration (1 scan for the 250k
  sample + 1 stream for the population); train is now 200k instead of 250k.
- **Caveat:** `TEST_SIZE=50_000` is absolute — the per-state sample must exceed
  50k (≥11 states at 5k/state), always true for the 50-state PUMS data but it
  would error on a tiny pool.
- **Verified end-to-end** on a 260k-row synthetic parquet (50 states × 5200,
  real 529-col schema) at `--iterations 1 --n-iter 1–2` for all three drivers:
  `_test` runs carry 10 metrics (unweighted+weighted ×5), `_population` runs
  carry 5 (unweighted), labeled parquets are 50k×5. **Not** run on the real 33M
  `data/test.parquet` yet — do a real cycle (keep the APFS `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI`)
  and watch population-stream wall time before trusting numbers.

### 2026-08-04 — remove Docker; rewrite README for the host-venv flow
- **User directive:** get rid of the Docker implementation and change the
  README to spell out how to run each model with its params + how to boot
  mlflow.
- **Deleted `Dockerfile`.** `.dockerignore` was already gone; `requirements.txt`
  is kept as the venv dependency list (no longer "used by the image"). Docker
  had drifted out of use and the new APFS default store
  (`~/mlruns_ml_pipeline`, see entry below) wouldn't exist inside a container
  anyway. Restore from git history if containerization is ever wanted again.
- **`README.md` rewritten** around `.venv/bin/python`: Setup (venv + `pip
  install -r requirements.txt`), data processing, a per-model run block
  (`logistic_regression.py` / `xgb.py` / `neural_network.py` each with
  `--iterations 5` and their default `--n-iter` — 20 for LR/XGB, 15 for NN),
  a flags table (`--iterations`, `--n-iter`, `--threshold`, positional
  `input`), and an MLflow-UI section pointing `--backend-store-uri` at the
  APFS store on port 5001.
- **CLAUDE.md body de-Dockered:** status line, Layout, "Running it", the
  "Docker is back" decision, and the Docker "Don't" rules all updated to the
  host-venv reality. Historical changelog entries left verbatim per the rule.
- No code changed; the three drivers already default to the APFS store.

### 2026-08-04 — all three drivers: default MLflow store → APFS (fixes ExFAT `._*` crash)
- **Trigger:** a plain `python3 logistic_regression.py --iterations 3
  data/test.parquet` (no `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI` exported) crashed with
  `UnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xb0` from
  `file_store.py` `_get_all_tags` → `read_file`. Root cause is the recurring
  AppleDouble quirk: the default store was `ml_pipline/mlruns/` on the ExFAT
  drive, macOS keeps regenerating `._*` sidecars there, and MLflow's file
  store reads each one as a tag file and chokes on the `0xb0` header byte.
  27 `._*` files were present under `mlruns/`; deleting them is not durable
  (recreated on the next write).
- **Fix:** changed the `TRACKING_URI` fallback in `logistic_regression.py`,
  `xgb.py`, `neural_network.py` from
  `f"file://{Path(__file__).resolve().parent / 'mlruns'}"` to the APFS store
  `"file:///Users/dpham/mlruns_ml_pipeline"` — the same escape hatch the
  earlier changelog entries told callers to export by hand, now the default.
  The `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI` env override still wins, so nothing that already
  sets it changes. New runs land alongside the existing July/August APFS runs
  instead of the stranded ExFAT `mlruns/`.
- **Docker caveat:** inside the container the old default resolved to the
  bind-mounted `/app/mlruns`; the new absolute host path
  `/Users/dpham/mlruns_ml_pipeline` does not exist in the container. If a
  driver is ever run inside Docker, export `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI` (e.g. to
  `/app/mlruns`) or runs won't persist to a bind mount. Host runs are the
  common path and are unaffected.
- `Path` is still used elsewhere in each file (import stays live). `py_compile`
  clean on all three; not re-run end-to-end.

### 2026-08-04 — xgb.py + neural_network.py: cast feature matrix to Float32 (match LR)
- **User directive:** apply the LR Float32 change to the other two drivers.
  `X` (train) and `X_b` (test batch) now `.cast(pl.Float32).to_numpy()`.
- **`xgb.py`:** was a bare `.to_numpy()` (Float64) — same ~1 GB → ~0.5 GB train
  drop as LR. Bonus: XGBoost's `hist` uses Float32 internally, so passing
  Float32 also avoids an internal up/down-conversion copy in `.fit()`.
- **`neural_network.py`:** was `.to_numpy().astype(np.float32)` — the *result*
  was already Float32, but it materialized a Float64 numpy array first, then
  cast. Casting inside polars removes that transient Float64 peak. Matters most
  here: the full matrix is moved to-device once (`X.to(device)`), so the
  host-side Float64 spike was doubling the resident copy right before the MPS
  transfer.
- `py_compile` clean on both; not run end-to-end.

### 2026-08-04 — logistic_regression.py: cast feature matrix to Float32
- **User directive** after an OOM crash: halve the train-matrix footprint.
  `X` (train) and `X_b` (test batch) now `.cast(pl.Float32).to_numpy()` instead
  of a bare `.to_numpy()`, which was promoting the mixed UInt8/Float32 frame to
  **Float64**. Cast happens *inside polars* so no transient Float64 numpy array
  is ever materialized. Train matrix (250K × 526) drops ~1 GB → ~0.5 GB; each
  50K test batch halves too. sklearn's LR upcasts internally for lbfgs, but the
  script-held copy + `train_test_split` copies are now half-size — that was the
  memory that got SIGKILL'd when a second pipeline ran concurrently.
- **Context:** the crash was an OOM kill (no traceback in logs), not a
  streaming failure — test set is still streamed via `pq.iter_batches`. Root
  cause was two+ pipelines (a manual foreground run + a stale LR→XGB→NN
  background run from an earlier session) hammering 18 GB RAM at once. Kill
  stragglers and run one model at a time; keep the APFS `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI`.
- **Not applied to `xgb.py` / `neural_network.py`** — same one-liner would help
  both (NN especially, since it holds the full matrix on-device), but only LR
  was requested. `py_compile` clean; not run end-to-end.

### 2026-08-04 — all three drivers: single-run train+test, per-state sampling, N iterations
- **User directive:** stop using `data/train.parquet`. Each program run now does
  training *and* evaluation itself (no more `--mode`). Per cycle: sample 5000
  rows per state out of `data/test.parquet` for training, evaluate on all the
  remaining rows. Training logs unweighted **and** weighted metrics; **test logs
  unweighted only**. New `--iterations` arg repeats the whole cycle, each with a
  fresh 5000-per-state sample.
- **`--mode` removed** from `logistic_regression.py`, `xgb.py`,
  `neural_network.py`. New `--iterations` flag (default 5). `--n-iter`
  (random-search trials) and `--threshold` unchanged. The positional `input` is
  now the *pool* to split (pass `data/test.parquet`), not a pre-split file.
- **Per-state sampling.** State is one-hot across 50 `State (FIPS code)_*`
  columns. Reconstruct an integer state id once up front via
  `pl.sum_horizontal([col_i * i ...])` (projection pushdown reads only the 50
  state cols), then `np.where` to build per-state row-index pools. Each
  iteration draws `min(5000, n_state)` indices per state with a per-iteration
  seed (`SEED + it`) → ~250K train rows. `PER_STATE = 5000` is a module
  constant (not a flag; user fixed it at 5000).
- **Train rows** loaded via `scan_parquet().with_row_index().filter(is_in(idx))`
  (one full scan, materializes only the 250K sample). **Test** streams the file
  in 50K-row pyarrow batches and drops sampled rows via a boolean
  `train_mask[offset:offset+len]` slice, so train/test are exactly disjoint and
  together cover the whole file. **Cost:** 2 full passes over the 6.7 GB file
  per iteration (scan for train + stream for test) — I/O heavy at high
  `--iterations`.
- **MLflow runs are flat + iteration-suffixed** (per user choice):
  `{variant}_iter{N}_train` (weighted+unweighted train metrics, `val_roc_auc`,
  `best_threshold`, nested `{variant}_iter{N}_trial_{i}` search runs) and
  `{variant}_iter{N}_test` (`test_unweighted_*` only). Run count per model per
  run = `iterations × 2 × (1 + n_iter + 1)` — lots of `._*` sidecar spray on
  ExFAT; keep the APFS `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI` escape hatch.
- **Model persistence dropped.** No more `mlflow.{sklearn,xgboost,pytorch}.log_model`
  / `search_logged_models` / `load_model` — models stay in memory since test
  runs in the same process. Those imports were removed.
- **Labeled output is per-iteration:** `<stem>_labeled_<model>_iter{N}.parquet`
  in `./data/`, same 5-column proba+binary schema. So a default 5-iteration run
  of all three models writes 15 labeled parquets.
- **Verified:** `py_compile` all three; a standalone check that the state-id
  reconstruction is exact and the train/test split is disjoint + complete
  (incl. the `n_state < 5000` cap); and an end-to-end `--iterations 2 --n-iter 2`
  run of all three on a 36K-row synthetic parquet — train runs carry both
  weighted+unweighted metrics, test runs carry unweighted-only, run names and
  per-iteration labeled parquets landed as specified. **Not** run on the real
  33M-row `data/test.parquet` yet — do a real cycle (keep exporting the APFS
  `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI`) before trusting numbers; watch wall time (2 full passes
  × iterations) and NN cost (full random search × iterations).
- **Follow-up — `create_figures.ipynb` is now stale against these runs.** It
  filters `attributes.run_name = '{variant}_test'` and reads
  `data/test_labeled_*.parquet`; the new run names are `{variant}_iter{N}_test`
  and the labeled files are `..._iter{N}.parquet`. The notebook needs updating
  (pick an iteration or aggregate across iterations) before regenerating figures.
  Not done here — not requested.

