### 2026-07-10 — `create_figures.ipynb`: Fig 1 clarify Δ label + grey unweighted row
- **User directive:** spell out what the Δ score is in the x-axis parenthesis,
  and make the top (unweighted-metrics) row grey.
- **X-axis label:** `$\Delta$ score (treatment - control)` →
  `$\Delta$ score (survey-weighted − non-survey-weighted)`. Names the actual
  contrast instead of the abstract treatment/control framing.
- **Top row greyed.** The `r == 0` (unweighted) row now forces all bars to
  `GREY` regardless of |Δ|; only the bottom (survey-weighted) row keeps the
  `ACCENT if |Δ| > 0.005 else GREY` rule. Reinforces the design language —
  the survey-weighted evaluation is the story, the unweighted row is context.
- **Not visually verified.** Open in Jupyter, run the Fig 1 cell.

### 2026-07-10 — all three drivers: log every random-search trial as a nested MLflow run
- **User directive:** log the individual trials (previously only the winning
  config was logged; losing trials were discarded in-memory).
- **Shape:** the per-variant `{variant}_train` run is now the **parent** and
  wraps the whole search + refit. Each trial opens a nested run
  `{variant}_trial_{i}` (`nested=True`) logging that trial's sampled
  hyperparameters (incl. the class-weighting hyperparameter — `class_weight`
  for LR, `use_class_weight`/`scale_pos_weight` for XGB,
  `use_class_weight`/`hidden`/`dropout`/`wd`/`lr` for NN) and its
  `val_roc_auc`. The parent still logs the best config, `val_roc_auc`, the
  full-data train metrics, and the model.
- **Run count per model:** `2 × (1 + n_iter)` training runs (parent +
  n_iter nested, per variant). At the NN default `n_iter=15` that's 32 runs
  per model — the file-store on the ExFAT drive will spray a lot more `._*`
  sidecars; keep using the APFS `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI` escape hatch.
- **Test-mode loading unaffected:** only the parent run logs a model (under
  `name=variant`); nested trial runs log no model, so
  `search_logged_models(filter_string="name='{variant}'")` still resolves to
  the one refit model per variant.
- **Verified end-to-end** (LR, `--n-iter 3`, synthetic parquet): 8 runs
  landed = 2 parents + 6 nested; each trial carried its `val_roc_auc` and
  the parent's value matched its best trial. XGB/NN share the identical
  structure and `py_compile` clean.

### 2026-07-10 — `neural_network.py`: drop SEARCH_EPOCHS, widen to depth-3 nets for 526 features
- **User directive:** get rid of the separate `SEARCH_EPOCHS` (use `EPOCHS`
  for both the search trials and the final refit), and resize the MLP for
  the real 526-feature input — depth 3, wider.
- **`SEARCH_EPOCHS` removed.** The random-search trial fits now train for the
  same `EPOCHS = 15` as the final refit (the trial-loop `train_mlp(...)` call
  now passes `EPOCHS`). One knob for both phases. **Cost note:** every trial
  is now a full 15-epoch fit, so the NN search is ~2× the compute it was at
  8 search-epochs — dial `--n-iter` down if that bites. LR/XGB were
  unaffected (no epoch concept; XGB's `n_estimators` stays a searched param).
- **Architecture widened to depth-3.** `HIDDEN_CHOICES` was
  `[(64,32),(128,64),(128,64,32)]` (mixed depth 2/3, max width 128) → now
  `[(256,128,64),(384,192,96),(512,256,128)]` — all three hidden layers,
  tapering, first layer 256–512 to match the ~526-dim one-hot+scaled input
  (confirmed: `data/test.parquet` = 529 cols − TARGET/TARGET_NEG/WEIGHT =
  526 features). Width stays a searched hyperparameter; depth is now fixed
  at 3.
- **Verified** `py_compile` + an `--n-iter 2` train+test smoke run on the
  600-row synthetic parquet (wide net on 6 synthetic features is
  overparameterized but exercises the code path): no errors, 5-column
  labeled output intact. **Not** run on the real 250K/33M parquets — the
  wider nets + full-epoch search will be noticeably slower there; do a real
  train+test cycle before trusting tuned numbers.

### 2026-07-10 — `create_figures.ipynb`: add Fig 4 calibration (reliability + ECE)
- **User directive:** add a calibration cell (follows the proba-column work
  below). New markdown `## Figure 4` + code cell appended after the Fig 3
  confusion-matrix cell.
- **What it shows:** 1×3 reliability diagrams (one panel per model),
  `calibration_curve(..., n_bins=10, strategy="quantile")` per variant —
  standard in `GREY`, survey-weighted in the `mako` `ACCENT`, grey dashed
  `y=x` perfect-calibration reference. Each variant's **ECE** (10 uniform
  bins, count-weighted) is direct-labeled in the legend, per the design
  notes' prefer-direct-labels rule. `set_box_aspect(1)` keeps each panel
  square so the 45° reference reads correctly.
- **Hard dependency:** reads `predicted_proba_{variant}` from the `labeled`
  dict (loaded in the Fig 2 cell). **The labeled parquets on disk are still
  the old binary-only 3-column files** — this cell raises `ColumnNotFound`
  until each model's `--mode test` is re-run with the updated drivers (see
  entry below). Cell has a comment saying so.
- **Design choice — unweighted calibration.** The reliability curve + ECE
  are computed on the test sample as-is (not population-weighted). Cleanest,
  most conventional read; a population-weighted reliability diagram (bins
  weighted by `State population`) is the natural follow-up if a reviewer
  asks "calibrated w.r.t. which population?".
- **Verified** the `calibration_curve` return order (`frac_pos, mean_pred`)
  and the `ece` helper on a 20k-row synthetic `(y, prob)` — no errors,
  sensible ECE. **Not** rendered in Jupyter and **not** run against real
  proba columns yet (they don't exist on disk until a re-test). Open in
  Jupyter after re-running test mode; spot-check the quantile binning
  doesn't collapse to too few points on the low-probability mass (disability
  base rate ~12%, so probs cluster low — quantile strategy handles this, but
  ties can merge bins, as seen in the synthetic check where 10 bins → 8
  points).

### 2026-07-10 — all three model drivers: proba+binary output, random search (class weighting is a hyperparameter)
- **User directive:** (1) labeled test outputs must carry *both* the
  `predict_proba` output and the binary predictions; (2) set up all three
  drivers for random-search hyperparameter tuning in which **use of class
  weighting is itself a searched hyperparameter**.
- **Output schema change (test mode).** The labeled parquet now has **5
  columns** (was 3): `TARGET`, plus per variant
  `predicted_proba_{variant}` (Float32 probability) and
  `predicted_disability_{variant}` (Int8 binary at `--threshold`). Ordered
  proba-then-binary per variant. This unblocks the calibration/ECE figure
  that was stalled on binary-only outputs (see Fig-1 entry below). Any
  consumer reading the old 3-col schema still finds the two binary columns
  under the same names.
- **Random search (re-introduces a stratified val split — reverses the
  2026-07-02 "reduce complexity" one-shot-fit decision, per explicit user
  directive).** Per variant: `train_test_split(..., test_size=0.1,
  stratify=y, random_state=42)`, draw `--n-iter` random configs (seeded
  `np.random.default_rng(42)`), score each on the val split by ROC-AUC —
  **survey-weighted ROC-AUC for the `survey_weighted` variant, unweighted
  for `standard`** — keep the best, then **refit the best config on the
  full input** and log that model. One MLflow run per variant as before,
  now with a `val_roc_auc` metric and the best hyperparameters as params
  (`search="random"`, `n_iter`).
- **Class weighting as a hyperparameter — per model:**
  - **LR** (`logistic_regression.py`): `class_weight ∈ {"balanced", None}`
    plus `C ~ log-uniform[1e-3, 1e1]`.
  - **XGB** (`xgb.py`): `use_class_weight ∈ {True, False}` toggles
    `scale_pos_weight` between the balanced `neg/pos` value and `1.0`; also
    searches `n_estimators∈[50,300]`, `max_depth∈[3,10]`, `learning_rate`
    log-uniform[0.01,0.32], `subsample`/`colsample_bytree`∈[0.6,1.0].
  - **NN** (`neural_network.py`): `use_class_weight ∈ {True, False}` toggles
    whether the balanced `compute_sample_weight` factor is folded into the
    per-sample loss weights; also searches `hidden ∈ {(64,32),(128,64),
    (128,64,32)}`, `dropout∈[0.1,0.5]`, `weight_decay` log-uniform[1e-5,
    1e-3], `lr` log-uniform[1e-4,~3e-3]. Search trials use
    `SEARCH_EPOCHS=8`; the final refit uses `EPOCHS=15`. Refactored the
    training loop into `train_mlp(...)` reused by both search trials and the
    final fit; `MLP.__init__` now takes explicit `hidden`/`dropout` (no
    module-level `HIDDEN`/`DROPOUT` constants).
  - Survey weighting is **orthogonal** to the class-weight hyperparameter:
    the `survey_weighted` variant still multiplies in `State population`
    (via `sample_weight` for LR/XGB, via the loss-weight tensor for NN)
    regardless of the sampled `use_class_weight`/`class_weight`.
- **New CLI flag `--n-iter`** (default: LR/XGB 20, NN 15) on all three, so
  search cost is tunable without editing the file. NN is the expensive one
  (each trial is a full 8-epoch fit × 2 variants) — dial `--n-iter` down
  there first.
- **Verified** via `py_compile` (all three) **and** an end-to-end smoke run
  of train+test for all three on a 600-row synthetic parquet (`--n-iter
  3–4`): no errors, and each wrote the 5-column labeled parquet with the
  proba(Float32)+binary(Int8) schema above. **Not** run on the real 250K
  train / 33M test parquets — do a real train+test cycle (keep exporting
  `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI=file:///Users/dpham/mlruns_ml_pipeline`, the APFS
  store) before trusting the tuned numbers, and re-run `create_figures.ipynb`
  after so it reads the freshly-tuned runs + new proba columns.

