### 2026-07-02 — `create_figures.ipynb` design pass per user's data-viz notes
- **Trigger:** user said the figures looked "AI" and pointed me at their Obsidian
  Zettelkasten note tree rooted at `~/Documents/Notes/3 - Zettelkasten/Data
  Visualization.md`. The linked notes lay out a Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic style
  framework: exploratory vs explanatory analysis, choosing effective visuals,
  clutter/cognitive load, preattentive attributes, design thinking,
  storytelling with data.
- **Diagnosis of the old notebook:** it was exploratory-dump-style — six figures
  covering every angle (results grid, delta, positive-rate, agreement,
  confusion matrices, disagreement diagnostics), all using the same mako-pair
  palette uniformly, all bars labeled, formal-but-empty titles ("Figure 1 —
  Test-set performance"). No visual hierarchy, no story arc, no strategic
  color, no direct labels.
- **What changed:**
  - **Cut from 6 to 4 figures.** Dropped the prediction-agreement heatmap and
    the disagreement diagnostics — both were exploratory ("here's another way
    to slice it") rather than explanatory. Pearls, not opened oysters.
  - **Slopegraph replaces the ROC-AUC bar chart** (Fig 1). The Zettelkasten's
    Choosing Effective Visuals note calls out slopegraphs as the right chart
    when comparing exactly two conditions across a small set of categories —
    line direction + length encode sign and magnitude directly, no
    subtraction required. Also sidesteps the "bars must start at zero"
    tension for a bounded metric like AUC where 0.5 is the chance floor.
  - **Grey + one mako accent, not all-mako.** Per Preattentive Attributes:
    grey as the default for context, one strategic color to highlight the
    treatment being evaluated (`ACCENT = mako[2]`). Standard variant is the
    reference (grey); survey_weighted is the intervention under evaluation
    (mako accent). Fig 2 additionally greys out delta bars where |Δ| < 0.005
    so the eye is drawn to the meaningful ones.
  - **Big Idea titles.** Each figure now has a complete-sentence title
    stating the finding, not just the topic. Fig 1: "Survey-weighted fitting
    reduces ROC-AUC across all three models." Fig 3: "Survey-weighted
    fitting nudges predictions toward the population base rate."
    Interpretation lives in these titles and the markdown captions; the plots
    themselves are quiet.
  - **Direct labels over legends where practical.** Fig 3 puts "standard"
    and "survey-weighted" text labels in the accent colors directly on the
    plot instead of a legend the eye has to keep referencing. Fig 1 labels
    the two endpoints and the model names on the plot itself.
  - **Chart-junk reduction.** Removed top/right spines globally. Left spine
    off on horizontal bar plots and slopegraphs (baseline is the value axis,
    not the category axis). Y-axis grid on the slopegraph only, in
    `#e6e6e6`. Tick marks removed from categorical axes.
  - **Font sizes tuned smaller.** `context=\"paper\"` with `font_scale=1.0`.
    Annotations dropped to 7.5–9pt to reduce the visual noise of the old
    version's `bar_label(fmt=\"%.2f\", fontsize=8)` on every bar.
  - **Kept:** `mako` colormap on Fig 4's confusion-matrix heatmaps (natural
    fit — sequential data, sequential palette). Row-normalized to percent so
    the six matrices are directly comparable.
- **File count is `figures/fig{1..4}_*.{pdf,png}`** (8 files after a run).
  Fig-1 through Fig-4 numbering matches the paper draft's figure order.
- **Structural changes for future edits to `create_figures.ipynb`:**
  - Do NOT reintroduce the deleted panels without a specific narrative reason.
    "Comprehensive" isn't a narrative reason — this is explanatory analysis,
    not a data dump.
  - Do NOT default to all-mako or all-colorful. Grey is the base state; mako
    accent is reserved for the treatment being evaluated. Same color = same
    meaning across the four figures.
  - Do NOT restore per-bar `bar_label` on every element. Only label values
    that matter to the story.
  - Complete-sentence titles state findings; markdown captions above each
    figure explain method and interpretation. Do not merge the two.
- **Still not smoke-tested end-to-end.** Same as the earlier attempt today,
  user's channel signal is "don't run this headless — open it in Jupyter." So
  the notebook JSON validates but figure rendering (label positioning,
  slopegraph endpoint annotation collisions, tight_layout warnings) has not
  been visually verified. Open in Jupyter, run all cells, spot-check
  `figures/fig{1..4}.pdf` before dropping into LaTeX.

### 2026-07-02 — model simplification + `create_figures.ipynb` for paper
- **User directive after two tuning passes plateau'd around AUC 0.65:** "reduce
  complexity of the 3 models." Ran the analysis, confirmed the improvements
  from val-split/early-stop/hyperparameter sweeps landed marginal test-set
  gains (\<0.005 AUC on top of the "improvements pass" v1 numbers). Rolled
  back the val-split ceremony to fixed-hyperparameter one-shot fits that
  match what tuning had settled on.
- **`logistic_regression.py`:** dropped `C_GRID` sweep + stratified val
  split + refit. Single `C = 0.01` (that was the tuned pick anyway) applied
  to both variants. **149 lines** (was 187).
- **`xgb.py`:** dropped stratified val split, `eval_set`, `early_stopping_rounds`,
  `reg_alpha`, `reg_lambda`, `min_child_weight` custom, learning_rate=0.1
  (was 0.05), `n_estimators = 100` (was 1000 with early stop, which typically
  picked ~100). `eval_metric` back to `logloss`. **179 lines** (was 220).
- **`neural_network.py`:** dropped val split, val-loss tracking, best-state
  snapshotting, patience-based early stopping. Smaller network `HIDDEN =
  (64, 32)` (was 128,64,32), fixed `EPOCHS = 15` (matches what val-loss
  early stop picked), `DROPOUT = 0.3` (down from 0.5 — smaller net doesn't
  overfit as hard, so less dropout needed). **228 lines** (was 253).
- **Test-set impact:** LR unchanged (AUC 0.655 std / 0.617 sw), XGB
  standard dropped 0.008 (0.653 → 0.645), NN essentially flat (0.646 →
  0.645 std, 0.642 → 0.636 sw). All three now cluster within 0.02 of
  AUC 0.65. **The tuning ceremony was not load-bearing for the results
  observed at this feature-engineering budget.** If future feature work
  (interaction terms, target-encoded high-card cats) pushes the ceiling up,
  the tuning may become worthwhile again — restore from git history.
- **New: `create_figures.ipynb`.** Publication-focused notebook that
  regenerates six figures from the latest MLflow test runs plus the labeled
  test parquets:
  - Fig 1 — per-metric bar grid (row=model, col=weighting scheme).
  - Fig 2 — Δ(standard − survey_weighted) per metric per model.
  - Fig 3 — positive-prediction rate, unweighted vs population-weighted.
  - Fig 4 — 2×2 prediction-agreement heatmap per model.
  - Fig 5 — 3×2 confusion matrix grid.
  - Fig 6 — on disagreement rows, who is right.
  All figures use seaborn's `mako` palette (two shades for the standard/
  survey-weighted contrast, three shades for the model contrast, `mako`
  colormap for heatmaps). Saved to `figures/` as both `.pdf` (vector, for
  LaTeX inclusion) and `.png` (300 dpi raster preview).
- **MLflow query pattern in the notebook:** uses
  `filter_string="attributes.run_name = '<variant>_test'"` with
