### 2026-07-02 — regularization pass on all three models + new APFS mlruns store
- **User directive:** "improve the three models and rerun train and test." Previous
  test-set results showed catastrophic overfitting in NN (train AUC 0.95 → test
  0.55), moderate overfitting in XGB (0.74 → 0.63), clean but plateau'd LR (0.66
  → 0.63). Each script now uses a stratified 90/10 train/val split for early
  stopping / hyperparameter selection; same seed (`SEED=42`) so the split is
  reproducible per script.
- **`neural_network.py`:** added `DROPOUT=0.3` between hidden layers,
  `WEIGHT_DECAY=1e-4` on the Adam optimizer, and — critically — **early stopping
  on val loss, not train loss.** The prior `EARLY_STOP_PATIENCE=10` on train
  loss did nothing to prevent overfit because train loss monotonically decreases
  even as val loss climbs. New loop tracks `best_val_loss`, snapshots the
  matching `state_dict`, restores it at end of training, and logs `val_loss` +
  `train_loss` per epoch to mlflow. Both variants early-stopped in ~17–19
  epochs — val loss started rising almost immediately, confirming the model had
  been badly overfit before.
- **`xgb.py`:** switched to `early_stopping_rounds=30` on a held-out val set via
  `eval_set` + `sample_weight_eval_set`. Bumped `n_estimators=1000` (ceiling)
  and dropped `learning_rate=0.05` (was 0.1). Added `min_child_weight=5`,
  `reg_alpha=0.1`, `reg_lambda=1.0`. `eval_metric` changed to `auc` so early
  stopping targets the same metric we care about downstream. Both variants
  early-stopped: standard @ 116 iterations, survey_weighted @ 25 (survey-
  weighted eval AUC saturated fast — weighting concentrates gradient signal on
  a few high-pop rows, easy to overfit).
- **`logistic_regression.py`:** added a `C ∈ {0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0}` sweep on
  the val split, pick best by val ROC-AUC, refit on the full train set with the
  chosen `C`. Everything else unchanged. Standard picked `C=0.01` (heavy
  regularization); survey_weighted picked `C=0.1`.
- **Test results (all on `data/test.parquet`, 33.17M rows, threshold=0.5):**
  | Model | Variant | ROC-AUC (u) prev → new | Δ | F1 (u) prev → new | Recall (u) prev → new |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | LR | standard | 0.631 → **0.655** | +0.024 | 0.248 → 0.274 | 0.339 → 0.465 |
  | LR | survey_weighted | 0.615 → 0.618 | +0.003 | 0.235 → 0.239 | 0.335 → 0.353 |
  | XGB | standard | 0.628 → 0.653 | +0.025 | 0.249 → 0.272 | 0.356 → 0.516 |
  | XGB | survey_weighted | 0.620 → 0.647 | +0.027 | 0.236 → 0.268 | 0.303 → 0.507 |
  | NN | standard | 0.546 → **0.645** | **+0.099** | 0.139 → 0.267 | 0.131 → 0.445 |
  | NN | survey_weighted | 0.535 → 0.642 | **+0.107** | 0.135 → 0.266 | 0.122 → 0.462 |

  NN went from near-random to competitive with LR/XGB; the win is entirely from
  val-loss early stopping (dropout + weight decay help but the loop *has* to
  stop at ~epoch 8–10 or the model collapses). All three models are now
  clustered around AUC 0.64–0.66 — the ceiling on this feature set with the
  current pipeline. Further gains likely need feature engineering (interaction
  terms, target-encoded high-cardinality cats we dropped) or a different model
  family.
- **New runs landed in `$HOME/mlruns_ml_pipeline`, not `ml_pipeline/mlruns/`.**
  The AppleDouble `._*` recreation-on-write on the ExFAT drive crashed mlflow
  reads with `UnicodeDecodeError` mid-run even after cleaning sidecars (macOS
  regenerates them on every write). Fell back to the documented escape hatch:
  `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI=file:///Users/dpham/mlruns_ml_pipeline` for the whole
  session (train + test). **This means the previous runs in
  `ml_pipeline/mlruns/` are stranded on the external drive and the current
  results only live in APFS.** Future train/test cycles need to keep exporting
  the same env var, or migrate the APFS store back to the external drive once
  a workaround for AppleDouble is found. The tracking URI default in the three
  scripts still points at `ml_pipeline/mlruns/` — override each invocation.
- **Voice note for future edits to these three drivers:** the user's terse
  preference caught up with me on the inline `# comments` explaining constants
  during the 2026-06-25 NN speedup pass (trimmed post-edit). This time I kept
  brief inline comments only where they explain *why* (the val-split rationale,
  the pre-computed min/max reasoning-analog for XGB early stopping). If those
  get trimmed too, drop them going forward and keep the why in the Changelog.

### 2026-06-25 — NN speedup pass + tqdm progress bars across all three drivers
- **`neural_network.py` train mode — speedup.** Aim: cut wall time on the Mac
  host venv without changing the model. Changes:
  - **MPS-aware device selection.** Added an `mps` branch between `cuda` and
    `cpu`, so Apple Silicon hosts use the GPU. Inside Docker (Linux on CPU)
    behavior is unchanged.
  - **Dropped `DataLoader` + `TensorDataset`.** Pure-Python per-batch
    iteration plus repeated `.to(device)` copies were the main per-step
    overhead at `BATCH_SIZE=1024`. Replaced with: full `X` / `y` / sample
    weights moved to device once, `torch.randperm(n, device=device)` each
    epoch for shuffle, manual index slicing per batch. With `Float32` X at
    250K × ~548 features that's ~548 MB resident on device — fits on MPS /
    CPU comfortably.
  - **`BATCH_SIZE` 1024 → 8192.** 8× fewer steps per epoch (244 → 31), much
    less Python overhead. `PREDICT_BATCH = 16_384` now controls inference
    batch size independently (was tied to `BATCH_SIZE * 4`).
  - **Early stopping on training-loss plateau.** Tracks the per-epoch mean
    loss; if no improvement of more than `EARLY_STOP_MIN_DELTA = 1e-4` for
    `EARLY_STOP_PATIENCE = 10` epochs, the loop breaks. `EPOCHS = 200` stays
    as the ceiling. Number of epochs actually trained is logged to mlflow
    as `trained_epochs`.
  - **Per-batch `loss.item()` removed from the inner loop** (was a forced
    GPU→CPU sync each step on MPS). Loss accumulated into a device-side
    scalar tensor and `.item()`'d once per epoch for the early-stop check
    and the tqdm postfix.
  - **Not verified end-to-end yet.** `py_compile` passes; rebuild the
    Docker image (new `tqdm` dep) and run `python neural_network.py --mode
    train data/train_processed.parquet` then `--mode test` to confirm.
- **`tqdm` progress bars added to all three model drivers.** Pinned in
  `requirements.txt` (unpinned version, resolves on first install).
  - **`neural_network.py`:** per-epoch bar in train mode (`desc=f"train
    {variant}"`, postfix shows current and best loss); per-parquet-batch
    bar in test mode (`desc="test nn"`, `total = ceil(num_rows /
    TEST_BATCH)` from `ParquetFile(...).metadata.num_rows`).
  - **`logistic_regression.py`:** `for variant in tqdm(VARIANTS, desc="train
    lr")` in train mode (sklearn's single `.fit()` has no per-iteration
    hook — variant-level is the only progress signal available); per-batch
    bar in test mode (`desc="test lr"`).
  - **`xgb.py`:** custom `TqdmCallback(xgb.callback.TrainingCallback)` ticks
    once per boosting round and closes the bar in `after_training`, wired
    via `XGBClassifier(callbacks=[TqdmCallback(total=200, desc=f"train xgb
    {variant}")])`. Per-batch bar in test mode (`desc="test xgb"`).
- **Voice cleanup after the edit:** explanatory inline comments I added on
  constant declarations (e.g. `EPOCHS = 200  # ceiling...`) were trimmed
  back to bare values by the user post-edit, consistent with the
  terse-voice preference. Future edits to these scripts: don't reach for
  inline `#` comments to explain constants; the changelog is the right
  place for the "why".

### 2026-06-25 — eda.ipynb: polars swap
- Swapped `pandas` → `polars` in the EDA notebook to match the rest of the
  pipeline's stack. Cell-by-cell:
  - Imports: `import pandas as pd` → `import polars as pl`.
  - Load: `pd.read_parquet(path)` → `pl.read_parquet(path)`.
  - Num/cat split: previously `df.describe().columns` (pandas trick to grab
    numeric cols) → `[c for c in df.columns if df[c].dtype.is_numeric()]`.
    `num_df = df.select(num_features)`, `cat_df = df.drop(num_features)`.
  - Rename uses polars' `df.rename({old: new, ...})` (dict, no `inplace`),
    filtered to keys actually present in `cat_df` so it doesn't `KeyError`
    on cols dropped upstream (e.g. high-card cats).
  - Correlation heatmap: `sns.heatmap(num_df.corr(), ...)` →
    `sns.heatmap(num_df.to_pandas().corr(), ...)`. Polars has `.corr()` but
    returns a label-less square frame; routing through `to_pandas().corr()`
    keeps the seaborn axis labels with one extra method call.
  - `cat_df.nunique().reset_index()` → explicit
    `pl.DataFrame({"feature": cat_df.columns, "n_unique": [cat_df[c].n_unique() ...]})`.
    Polars' `df.select(pl.all().n_unique()).transpose(...)` works too but
    reads more obscurely; the dict-constructor form matches the rest of
    the notebook's directness.
  - `value_counts(normalize=True)` → `value_counts(sort=True, normalize=True)`.
    Polars supports `normalize` on `Series.value_counts` as of 0.20.x;
    `sort=True` preserves the descending-by-count display order pandas gave
    by default.
- **Why:** user directive — align EDA with the polars-only stack used by
  `data_processing.py` and the model drivers; drops `pandas` as a runtime
  dep for this notebook (still needed transitively for the heatmap step).
- **Not re-executed.** Outputs in the notebook are from the prior pandas
  run and are now stale. Re-run cells in order against a valid input
  parquet to refresh them.

### 2026-06-25 — model scripts: descriptive labeled-output suffixes
- Test-mode output filenames now spell out the model used:
  - `logistic_regression.py`: `<stem>_labeled.parquet` →
    `<stem>_labeled_logistic_regression.parquet`
  - `xgb.py`: `<stem>_labeled_xgb.parquet` →
    `<stem>_labeled_xgboost.parquet`
  - `neural_network.py`: `<stem>_labeled_nn.parquet` →
    `<stem>_labeled_neural_network.parquet`
- **Why:** user wants the output filename to identify the model that
  produced the predictions at a glance, rather than relying on terse
  suffixes / no suffix at all.
- All three scripts continue to write into `./data/` and still produce the
  same three-column schema (`TARGET`, `predicted_disability_standard`,
  `predicted_disability_survey_weighted`).
- Any prior `<stem>_labeled.parquet` / `_labeled_xgb.parquet` /
  `_labeled_nn.parquet` files already on disk are not renamed; future test
  runs land at the new paths.

### 2026-06-25 — model scripts: streaming test mode (pyarrow row-batches)
- **All three model drivers** (`logistic_regression.py`, `xgb.py`,
  `neural_network.py`) now stream the input parquet in test mode instead of
  materializing the full feature matrix. Train mode is unchanged — sklearn
  LR, XGB `.fit()`, and the PyTorch training loop still need the full `X`
  in memory.
- **Why:** test on `_full.parquet` (and the upcoming larger splits) OOM'd
  because `pl.read_parquet(...).drop(...).to_numpy()` materialised the
  whole ~548-column dense matrix at once. At Float32 that's ~2.2 KB/row;
  on a 10M-row split that's ~22 GB before any model touches it.
- **Shape of the change:**
  1. Top-level `df = pl.read_parquet(...)` / `X = df.drop(...).to_numpy()`
     moved *inside* the `train` branch. Test branch never reads the full
     frame.
  2. Test branch loads both variants up front into a `loaded` dict, then
     iterates `pq.ParquetFile(args.input).iter_batches(batch_size=BATCH)`
     once. Per batch: convert to polars via
     `pl.from_arrow(pa.Table.from_batches([batch]))`, slice off the
     `(TARGET, TARGET_NEG, WEIGHT)` columns, `predict_proba` with each
     loaded model, append the 1D prob slice to a per-variant list.
  3. After the loop, `np.concatenate` the per-variant prob lists + `y` +
     `w`, then compute metrics and write the labeled parquet exactly as
     before.
- **Peak memory in test mode** is now roughly `BATCH × n_features × 4 B`
  (one batch of `X_b`) + `n_rows × 8 B × ~4` (y/w/prob arrays). At
  `BATCH=50_000` and 548 features that's ~110 MB per batch + ~1.6 GB of
  1D state at 50M rows — fits comfortably.
- **Batch size:** `BATCH = 50_000` in LR/XGB, `TEST_BATCH = 50_000` in NN
  (separate name to avoid clashing with the existing train-loop
  `BATCH_SIZE = 1024`). Tune larger if the host has headroom and the
  loader becomes I/O-bound; tune smaller if a single batch's X is still
  too wide.
- **Dependency:** `pyarrow` was already a pinned runtime dep (used
  transitively by polars + by `data_processing.py`'s sink); both scripts
  now import it explicitly as `pyarrow as pa` and `pyarrow.parquet as pq`
  at the top.
- **NN-specific:** `predict_proba(model, X_b)` still sub-batches into
  `BATCH_SIZE * 4 = 4096`-row chunks internally for the GPU forward
  pass. With file-level batching at 50K, that's just two layers of
  batching — harmless, no rewrite needed.
- **Metric semantics unchanged.** All five metrics (accuracy, precision,
  recall, F1, ROC-AUC) and both their unweighted / survey-weighted
  variants are still computed on the full concatenated `y` / `prob`
  arrays at the end of the loop, so test runs are bit-identical to the
  pre-streaming versions modulo floating-point accumulation order in the
  `concatenate`.
- **Output schema unchanged.** Same three columns (`TARGET`,
  `predicted_disability_standard`, `predicted_disability_survey_weighted`)
  written to the same `<input-stem>_labeled{,_xgb,_nn}.parquet` paths.
- **Train mode is untouched in shape and verified by `py_compile` only.**
  Run each script end-to-end after rebuilding (no Dockerfile change
  needed; pyarrow is already in `requirements.txt`) to confirm the test
  branch finishes within the new memory envelope.

### 2026-06-25 — data_processing.py: streaming lazy pipeline, one-hot from codes
- **Rewrote the pipeline as a single lazy plan ending in `sink_parquet`.** No
  more `read_parquet` → loop → `collect()` → `to_dummies()` → `write_parquet()`.
  Whole pipeline runs through polars' streaming engine.
- **Why:** OOM'd on the 3 GB `_full.parquet`. Eager replace+to_dummies
  materialised two giant frames in sequence (string-encoded labels, then the
  wide one-hot output).
- **Key changes:**
  - `scan_parquet` instead of `read_parquet`.
  - **One-hot from raw PUMS codes**, skipping the `replace` → `Categorical` →
    `to_dummies` detour. For each col in `VALUE_LABELS`, emit
    `(pl.col(c).cast(String) == code).cast(UInt8).alias(f"{COLUMN_LABELS[c]}_{label}")`.
    Column names match the previous pipeline so `TARGET = "Disability
    (recoded)_With a disability"` and friends still resolve.
  - Non-numeric columns outside `VALUE_LABELS` (e.g. `ST` if string-typed)
    handled via a small `unique().drop_nulls().implode()` pre-pass.
  - MinMax outputs cast to `Float32` (was `Float64`).
  - Compression: `lz4` (was the default). Faster writes; bigger file. Swap to
    `lz4_raw` if polars rejects `lz4` on this version.
- **Streaming-engine bailout fix.** `(col - col.min()) / (col.max() - col.min())`
  inside the main plan broadcasts an aggregation back across rows; polars 1.6's
  streaming engine doesn't support that, falls back to the standard engine,
  which then refuses `sink_parquet` with
  `InvalidOperationError: sink_Parquet ... not yet supported in standard engine`.
  **Fix:** pre-compute min/max scalars in a one-shot aggregation
  `lf.select([min/max exprs]).collect().row(0, named=True)`, then use them as
  Python literals in the scaling expression. Main plan stays pure per-row →
  streams cleanly.
- **Verified end-to-end** inside the Docker container on the 3 GB `_full.parquet`.
- **Body docs updated:** "Pipeline" section above rewritten to describe the
  new flow.

### 2026-06-25 — neural_network.py: PyTorch rewrite with survey-weighted BCE
- **Replaced `sklearn.neural_network.MLPClassifier` with a PyTorch MLP.** Same
  `(128, 64, 32)` hidden shape, single-logit output, same binary
  cross-entropy objective — but survey weights now enter the loss directly
  instead of via a weighted bootstrap.
- **Why:** sklearn's MLPClassifier accepts neither `sample_weight` nor
  `class_weight`. The previous version worked around this by resampling rows
  with `np.random.choice(p=...)` — effective in expectation but indirect, and
  noisy at large `n`.
- **Survey-weighted BCE** (`weighted_bce_loss`) lifted verbatim from
  `../../SDSI496/classifier.py`:
  ```python
  bce = F.binary_cross_entropy_with_logits(logits, targets, reduction="none")
  return (bce * sample_weights).sum() / sample_weights.sum()
  ```
  Per user directive, only this function was taken — the rest of that
  reference file (reservoir sampling, k-fold, sweep harness) is unrelated and
  not borrowed.
- **Variant semantics preserved.** Both variants get `compute_sample_weight
  ("balanced", y)` folded into the per-sample weight tensor, matching LR's
  `class_weight="balanced"` and XGB's `scale_pos_weight` behavior. The
  `survey_weighted` variant additionally multiplies by `State population`. The
  `standard` variant uses uniform population weights (only class balance).
- **MLflow swap:** `mlflow.sklearn.log_model / load_model` →
  `mlflow.pytorch.log_model / load_model`. Existing experiment dir still
  holds the old sklearn-era LoggedModels; test mode picks the most recent by
  `creation_timestamp DESC` as before, so the first PyTorch run will be the
  one loaded.
- **Hyperparams:** `EPOCHS=200`, `BATCH_SIZE=1024`, `LR=1e-3`, Adam. 200
  epochs in PyTorch is meaningfully more work than sklearn's `max_iter=200`
  (which early-stops on plateau by default) — expect noticeably longer
  training. Tune down or add validation-loss early-stop if it drags.
- **CUDA-aware** via `torch.device("cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else
  "cpu")`. No-op on the current Mac host; will activate inside a CUDA-enabled
  container without code changes.
- **`requirements.txt`:** `torch` added (unpinned, first install resolves the
  version).
- **Not yet verified end-to-end.** Rebuild the Docker image (new dep) and run
  `python neural_network.py --mode train ...` then `--mode test ...` before
  trusting it.

### 2026-06-25 — model scripts: output parquet is label + predictions only
- **All three model scripts** (`logistic_regression.py`, `xgb.py`,
  `neural_network.py`) now write only `{TARGET, predicted_disability_standard,
  predicted_disability_survey_weighted}` in test mode. The full feature
  frame is no longer copied through.
- **Was:** `df.with_columns([pl.Series(n, v) for n, v in
  out_cols.items()]).write_parquet(out)` — preserved every input feature
  plus the new predictions, producing a wide output.
- **Now:** `pl.DataFrame({TARGET: y, **out_cols}).write_parquet(out)` — three
  columns total.
- **Why:** features are losslessly reproducible from the input parquet;
  carrying them through bloated the labeled output and added nothing
  actionable. Predictions are joinable back to features via row order if a
  downstream consumer needs the full frame.
- **Backward compat:** any consumer expecting the old wide labeled parquet
  will need to re-attach features via row index. None known so far.

### 2026-06-25 — Dockerfile + filename: copy nn/xgb, logstic→logistic rename
- **Dockerfile `COPY` line** updated to include `neural_network.py` and
  `xgb.py` so all three model drivers run inside the container without
  bind-mounting.
- **File rename:** `logstic_regression.py` → `logistic_regression.py`. Done
  by the user out of band; the Dockerfile already reflects the new name. The
  earlier "typo'd `logstic_` is intentional" rule (2026-06-10 changelog
  entry) is **superseded** — only the directory typo (`ml_pipline`) remains
  intentional. Historical changelog entries that reference
  `logstic_regression.py` are kept verbatim per the entries-are-historical
  rule; new edits should use `logistic_regression.py`.
- **Rebuild required** to pick up both the new `COPY` line and the `torch`
  dependency. Code-only edits still iterable via bind mount (`-v
  $(pwd):/app`).

### 2026-06-15 — add xgb.py (XGBoost, control + treatment)
- **New file `xgb.py`**, structurally a mirror of `logstic_regression.py` per
  user directive ("same iterative code ... except for model specifics"). Same
  CLI (`--mode {train,test}`, `--threshold`, one positional `input`), same
  MLflow logging shape (`train_unweighted_*` / `train_weighted_*` /
  `test_unweighted_*` / `test_weighted_*` + matching param dicts), same
  model-loading path (LoggedModel name = variant, `search_logged_models`
  ordered by `creation_timestamp DESC`), same two variants (`standard`,
  `survey_weighted`). Control/treatment contrast is the iid-vs-survey-weighted
  framing from Kim (2020) — same as LR and NN scripts.
- **Filename is `xgb.py`, not `xgboost.py`.** User-confirmed after I flagged
  that `xgboost.py` would shadow the installed `xgboost` PyPI package on
  `python xgboost.py` invocation (`from xgboost import XGBClassifier` resolves
  to the script itself, recursive import). Don't rename to `xgboost.py`.
- **Model:** `xgboost.XGBClassifier(n_estimators=200, max_depth=6,
  learning_rate=0.1, tree_method="hist", objective="binary:logistic",
  eval_metric="logloss", n_jobs=-1, scale_pos_weight=...)`. Modest defaults,
  no tuning yet — left for future iteration.
- **Class imbalance via `scale_pos_weight`** computed as
  `(y == 0).sum() / max((y == 1).sum(), 1)`. This is XGBoost's analogue of
  LR's `class_weight="balanced"` — the per-positive gradient is multiplied by
  this factor, so the ~12% positive-rate target gets a ~7.2× lift. Logged as
  a param so it's traceable. Survey-weighted variant *also* passes
  `sample_weight=w` to `.fit()`, so the effective per-row weight is
  `state_population[i] * scale_pos_weight` on positives — same multiplicative
  stack as the LR script.
- **Persistence via `mlflow.xgboost`** (`log_model` / `load_model`) rather
  than `mlflow.sklearn`. Functionally parallel — both write LoggedModel
  entries under `mlruns/<exp>/models/m-*/` and load back via `models:/<id>`.
  `XGBClassifier` is still the sklearn-API wrapper, so `predict_proba` etc.
  behave identically to LR's interface.
- **Different experiment name:** `EXPERIMENT = "xgboost_survey_weights"` so
  XGBoost runs don't share the LR or NN experiment dir / model namespace.
  Test mode looks up models in this experiment only.
- **Different output suffix:** test mode writes
  `<input-stem>_labeled_xgb.parquet`, parallel to NN's `_labeled_nn.parquet`
  and LR's `_labeled.parquet`. All three can run on the same input without
  clobbering each other.
- **`requirements.txt` updated** — `xgboost` added (unpinned). The host venv
  doesn't have it installed yet; first `pip install` (or `docker build`)
  resolves the version, pin afterward to match the existing
  pinned-to-host-versions convention.
- **Not yet verified end-to-end.** Script is structurally a port of the LR
  driver — same CLI, same MLflow surface, same load path. Run inside the
  Docker container (`python xgb.py --mode train data/train_processed.parquet`
  then `--mode test`) after rebuilding the image to pick up the new
  `xgboost` dep. Should be fast — `tree_method="hist"` on 250K × ~548 with
  200 trees runs in a couple minutes per variant.

### 2026-06-15 — add neural_network.py (3-layer MLP, control + treatment)
- **New file `neural_network.py`**, structurally a mirror of
  `logstic_regression.py` per user directive ("same iterative code ... except
  for model specifics"). Same CLI (`--mode {train,test}`, `--threshold`, one
  positional `input`), same MLflow logging shape (`train_unweighted_*` /
  `train_weighted_*` / `test_unweighted_*` / `test_weighted_*` + matching
  param dicts), same model-loading path (LoggedModel name = variant,
  `search_logged_models` ordered by `creation_timestamp DESC`), same two
  variants (`standard`, `survey_weighted`).
- **Model:** `sklearn.neural_network.MLPClassifier(hidden_layer_sizes=(128, 64, 32),
  max_iter=200, random_state=42)`. Three hidden layers, adam solver (sklearn
  default), no extra hyperparam tuning yet — left for future iteration.
- **Survey-weight + class-balance handling via weighted bootstrap.** Unlike
  `LogisticRegression`, `MLPClassifier.fit()` accepts neither `sample_weight`
  nor `class_weight`. To get the same effect for both variants the script
  resamples rows with `np.random.default_rng(SEED).choice(n, size=n,
  replace=True, p=combined/combined.sum())` where `combined =
  compute_sample_weight("balanced", y) * (w if survey_weighted else 1)`.
  - Standard variant: equivalent in expectation to `class_weight="balanced"`
    on the original rows (no survey weights).
  - Survey-weighted variant: equivalent in expectation to
    `class_weight="balanced"` *and* `sample_weight=w` stacked the same way
    they stack in the LR script (`class_weight × sample_weight`). Faithful
    to Kim (2020) Eq. 1 generalization — treatment group uses survey
    weights for both fit and metric calc; control uses neither.
- **Different experiment name:** `EXPERIMENT = "neural_network_survey_weights"`
  so NN runs don't share the LR's experiment dir / model namespace. Test
  mode looks up models in this experiment only.
- **Different output suffix:** test mode writes
  `<input-stem>_labeled_nn.parquet` (LR writes `<input-stem>_labeled.parquet`)
  so both scripts can run on the same input without clobbering each other.
- **MLflow file-store quirks** (FAT32 / ExFAT AppleDouble, file-store
  maintenance-mode env var, tracking URI computation) are handled identically
  to `logstic_regression.py`. No new env knobs.
- **Not yet verified end-to-end.** Script is structurally a port of the LR
  driver — same CLI, same MLflow surface, same load path. Run inside the
  Docker container (`python neural_network.py --mode train
  data/train_processed.parquet` then `--mode test`) before relying on it.
  Heavier than LR — adam on 250K × ~548 features × 3 hidden layers will
  take noticeably longer per variant.

### 2026-06-15 — logstic_regression.py: class_weight=balanced + --threshold flag
- **Train mode: `LogisticRegression(class_weight="balanced", ...)` for both
  variants** (logstic_regression.py:74-76). The 12% positive-rate target
  was collapsing the model to the majority-class baseline (acc ≈ 0.879,
  recall ≈ 0.002 on the train set). `class_weight="balanced"` reweights
  the loss so each class contributes equally — effectively a ~7.2×
  multiplier on positive-class rows. Logged as a param so the change is
  traceable in mlflow.
- **For `survey_weighted`, `class_weight` and `sample_weight` stack
  multiplicatively** in sklearn. The effective per-row weight is now
  `state_population[i] * class_weight[y[i]]` — that's the intended
  behavior (population-representative *and* class-balanced fitting), but
  worth knowing if results look odd.
- **Test mode: new `--threshold FLOAT` flag** (default `0.5`). Replaces
  `clf.predict(X)` with `(clf.predict_proba(X)[:, 1] >= threshold)`, so
  the operating point is tunable post-hoc without retraining. Threshold
  is logged as a param on the test run so each evaluation is
  self-describing.
- **Verified end-to-end inside Docker** (script mounted live via `-v`):
  retrained both variants, then swept threshold ∈ {0.3, 0.5, 0.7} on the
  train parquet:
  | threshold | variant         | precision | recall | positives |
  |-----------|-----------------|-----------|--------|-----------|
  | 0.3       | standard        | 0.132     | 0.960  | 221,080   |
  | 0.5       | standard        | 0.184     | 0.607  | 100,144   |
  | 0.5       | survey_weighted | 0.176     | 0.639  | 109,827   |
  | 0.7       | standard        | 0.299     | 0.141  | 14,363    |
  Recall on the un-balanced baseline was ~0.002; the lift comes almost
  entirely from `class_weight="balanced"`, with `--threshold` giving
  smooth control between the precision/recall corners.
- **Old models still in `mlruns/`.** The pre-balanced LoggedModels from
  the earlier train remain in `mlruns/<exp>/models/`; test mode picks up
  the newest by `creation_timestamp DESC`, so it'll grab the balanced
  ones automatically. Delete the old `m-aec7f27.../` and `m-d644f16.../`
  dirs if you want a clean slate.
- **`README.md` updated** to show `--threshold 0.5` in the Run section
  as a hint that the flag exists; default is unchanged.

### 2026-06-15 — logstic_regression.py: train run_name -> {variant}_train
- Train mode now opens its mlflow run with `run_name=f"{variant}_train"`
  (was `run_name=variant`), so train/test runs are symmetric in the UI:
  `standard_train` / `standard_test`, `survey_weighted_train` /
  `survey_weighted_test`.
- **Safe re: model loading.** Test mode finds models by LoggedModel name
  (`name='{variant}'`, set on `mlflow.sklearn.log_model(clf, name=variant)`),
  not by run name — so renaming the run doesn't change how
  `search_logged_models` resolves the artifact. The 2026-06-11 model-loading
  fix is untouched.
- **Existing runs are not retroactively renamed.** The two runs already in
  `mlruns/` from the prior train (`standard`, `survey_weighted`) keep their
  original `run_name`. Only future train runs get the `_train` suffix.

### 2026-06-15 — mlflow UI port 5000 → 5001
- **Port 5000 was taken on the user's host** (likely macOS AirPlay
  Receiver, the usual culprit). Switched everything that references the
  mlflow UI port to **5001**: `Dockerfile` `EXPOSE`, the `docker run -p`
  flag and `mlflow ui --port` invocation in both `README.md` and the
  "Running it" block in this file, and the prose "port 5000 exposed"
  note. Prior changelog entries that mention 5000 are kept verbatim per
  the historical-entries rule.
- **Rebuild is not strictly required.** `EXPOSE` is documentation only;
  the actual binding is the `-p 5001:5001` flag on `docker run`, and
  `mlflow ui --port 5001` is what binds inside the container. The
  existing image still works as long as you pass the new `-p` and
  `--port` values. Rebuild only if you want the `EXPOSE` metadata in
  sync (e.g., for `docker inspect` / image-listing readability).

### 2026-06-15 — logstic_regression.py: log test-mode metrics to mlflow
- **Test mode now opens a new mlflow run per variant** (`{variant}_test` run
  name, same experiment as training) and logs the unweighted + weighted
  versions of accuracy / precision / recall / F1 / ROC-AUC under the
  `test_unweighted_*` / `test_weighted_*` prefixes — mirroring the train
  mode's `train_*` schema so the UI shows train vs test metrics on the
  same axes. Params logged: `variant`, `model_uri` (the `models:/...` URI
  that was loaded), `input`, `n_rows`, `n_features`. **Why:** user wants
  test-mode runs to be a record of held-out model evaluation, not just a
  prediction-writing convenience.
- **Moved `y = df[TARGET].to_numpy()` to the module-top loading block**
  (next to `X` and `w`). It was previously train-only; now both modes need
  it for metric computation. The script still requires the target column
  to be present in the input parquet — that was already true for train
  and is now enforced for test too. If you later need to score truly
  unlabeled data, the no-metrics path would need to come back as a
  separate flag.
- **Verified end-to-end** by mounting the live script into the container
  (`docker run -v ./logstic_regression.py:/app/logstic_regression.py
  ml_pipeline python logstic_regression.py --mode test
  data/train.parquet`) and confirming via `MlflowClient.search_runs` that
  `standard_test` and `survey_weighted_test` runs landed with all ten
  metrics each. Numbers matched the corresponding train runs because the
  test was run on the train parquet itself; they'll diverge on a
  held-out file.
- **Rebuild reminder:** the existing `ml_pipeline:latest` image is from
  the prior Dockerfile build and still has the old script COPY'd in. Run
  `docker build -t ml_pipeline .` to pick up this change, or mount the
  script via `-v` (as in the verification command above) for iteration.

### 2026-06-15 — README.md: add Run section with per-parquet command flow
- Added a "Run" section showing the four commands (preprocess → train → test
  → `mlflow ui`) to execute from the container shell against a parquet
  dropped into `./data/`. Build + Start sections unchanged. Style matches
  the prior pass: short, no per-flag prose, defers to `CLAUDE.md` for
  rationale.

### 2026-06-11 — fix test-mode model loading for MLflow 3 logged-model layout
- **Bug:** test mode crashed with `MlflowException: Failed to download
  artifacts from path 'model'`. `mlflow.sklearn.log_model(clf, name="model")`
  in MLflow 3 routes the model to the experiment-level
  `mlruns/<exp>/models/m-<id>/artifacts/` store as a first-class
  Logged Model, not under the run's `artifacts/`. The run dir only keeps a
  pointer in `outputs/m-<id>/meta.yaml`. The old `runs:/<run_id>/model` URI
  resolves to `<run.artifact_uri>/model` (= `<run>/artifacts/model`), which
  is empty under this layout — hence the load failure.
- **Fix:** log each variant under its variant name
  (`mlflow.sklearn.log_model(clf, name=variant)`) so the two LoggedModels
  are distinguishable, and load in test mode via
  `client.search_logged_models(experiment_ids=[...], filter_string=f"name='{variant}'", order_by=[{"field_name":"creation_timestamp","ascending":False}], max_results=1)`
  → `models:/{model.model_id}`. Hard-errors with `SystemExit` if no
  LoggedModel of that name exists, matching the existing experiment-missing
  behavior.
- **Why the prior changelog's "Verified end-to-end on synthetic data" entry
  did not catch this:** kept verbatim per the entries-are-historical rule,
  but treat it as superseded. Current end-to-end was re-verified after this
  fix against the real 250K-row `data/train.parquet` (train logs two
  variants → test loads `models:/m-...` → writes `data/train_labeled.parquet`
  with both `predicted_disability_*` columns). The full
  `docker build → docker run train → docker run test → mlflow ui` flow is
  now working from the bind-mounted `./mlruns/`.
- **Observed but not fixed (separate concern):** both variants predict the
  positive class on only ~0.05% of rows vs ~12% actual positive rate, so
  the train-set "accuracy" of 0.879 is just the majority-class rate. Likely
  a class-imbalance / regularization issue (default `lbfgs`, no
  `class_weight`); not a load-path bug. Worth investigating separately
  before treating these models as useful.
- **AppleDouble note:** `._MLmodel`, `._model.pkl`, `._conda.yaml`, etc.
  reappear inside the freshly-written `models/m-*/artifacts/` directory the
  moment the container writes to the ExFAT bind mount. Not blocking
  loading via `models:/` (mlflow looks for `MLmodel` by exact name), but it
  will trip `mlflow ui` if/when it scans artifacts. Quirk 1 cleanup mantra
  still applies; the `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI=file://$HOME/...` escape hatch
  remains the documented fallback.

### 2026-06-11 — add README.md (Docker-focused user guide)
- **New file `README.md`** at user request: build, start, run scripts +
  `mlflow ui`, rebuild after edits. Initially written long with per-flag
  explanations and an end-to-end flow block; user pushed back to simplify
  and de-AI-ify, so the current version drops the "What's in the image"
  section (versions live in `requirements.txt`), the flag-by-flag
